{"schema":1,"id":"monty-hall","title":"Why does the host’s rule change the odds of switching?","lang":"en","sources":["https://mathigon.org/course/probability/monty-hall","https://doi.org/10.1080/00031305.1975.10479121"],"settings":[{"id":"host","label":"Host rule","default":"informed","options":[{"value":"informed","label":"Knows where the car is; opens a goat at random"},{"value":"random","label":"Opens a random unchosen door"}]}],"answer":"Switching wins 2 in 3 if the host knows where the car is and picks at random between two goats, but 1 in 2 if he opened a random door and showed a goat","checks":[{"question":"This host knows where the car is. When your first pick is right, he opens one of the two goats at random. When it is wrong, he tosses a coin: heads, he opens the car and the round ends; tails, he opens the other goat. You pick A and he opens B to a goat. How often does switching win, and which strip in the last step is his?","answer":"50.0%. His strip is the random host’s, cell for cell: with the car behind B or C, he opens each unchosen door half the time, so one of the two sixths in each wrong-pick third shows the car. He knows where the car is, but his rule gives each round the random host’s chance, and those chances set the odds."},{"question":"The producers hide the car behind A 40% of the time and behind B or C 30% each. You pick A and the host opens B to a goat. How often does switching win with the host who knows, and with the random host?","answer":"With the host who knows, 60.0%: the car-behind-A rounds now fill 40% of the strip and he opens B in half of them, 20%, while all of the car-behind-C rounds, 30%, open B. With the random host, 42.9%: he opens B in only half of the car-behind-C rounds, 15%, against the same 20%."}],"cases":[{"values":{"host":"informed"},"name":"Host rule: Knows where the car is; opens a goat at random","duration":106.2,"paper":"#ffffff","steps":[{"step":"choice","title":"You pick A; the host opens B: a goat","line":"Sho